#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078339 :question/id #uuid "1c7b0878-a386-5449-9ff4-c37c8cda1b92" :question/math-academy-id "e-6589" :question/problem "Given that $y = y(x)$ satisfies the equation $y''+7y'+6y = 0,$ which of the following could **not** be $y(x)?$\n\nI. $-3e^{-6x}$\n\nII. $2e^{-6x}-4e^{-x}$\n\nIII. $e^{-6x}+2e^{x}$" :question/worked-solution "This is a second-order homogeneous ODE. So, we assume the solutions take the form $y=e^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\ny'= \\lambda e^{\\lambda x},\\qquad y''= \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+7\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+6{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+7\\lambda +6) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+7\\lambda +6 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +6)(\\lambda +1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe roots of the characteristic equation are $\\lambda = -6$ and $\\lambda = - 1.$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{-6x} + Be^{- x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to determine whether each of the given functions takes the form shown above.\n\n- Function I takes the above form with $A=-3$ and $B=0.$ So, it is a solution to the given ODE.\n\n- Function II takes the above form with $A=2$ and $B=-4.$ So, it is a solution to the given ODE.\n\n- Function III does *not* take the above form. It contains $e^x,$ which is not included in the above form. So, it is *not* a solution to the given ODE.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f271e83c-eb95-5d23-8e02-0039c4c92647" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086047} {:answer/id #uuid "01c305ef-5d62-5370-af62-e640c51bd827" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-1$ or $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086048} {: