 :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y = e^{x}(P\\cos (2x) + Q\\sin (2x))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086889} {:answer/id #uuid "3c7a92f1-25f9-533a-a3dc-40f0e0758abd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y = P\\cos (2x) + Q\\sin (2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086890}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186086886} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5696827-5828-507d-a912-d923a169beb3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086885}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087596 :question/id #uuid "b22fa1be-4c82-5dbf-b5fe-5cbb42c0c646" :question/math-academy-id "e-6596" :question/problem "Given that $y = y(t)$ satisfies the equation $y'' - 4y' + 85y = 0,$ which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?\n\nI. $-2e^{2t}\\cos{9t} + 7e^{2t}\\sin{9t}$\n\nII. $e^{2t}\\cos{9t}+3e^{2t}\\sin{3t}$\n\nIII. $-6e^{2t}\\sin{9t}$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y = e^{\\lambda t}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\ny' = \\lambda e^{\\lambda t}, \\qquad y'' = \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda t} .\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}-4\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t}+85{e}^{\\lambda t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda t}({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +85) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 - 4\\lambda + 85 = 0,\n$$\n\nand we can solve it by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +85 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +4)-4+85 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)^{2}+81 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)^{2} & =-81 \\\\ \\lambda -2 & =\\pm 9\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =2\\pm 9\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\lambda = 2 \\pm 9 \\textrm{i}$ are the complex roots of the characteristic equation, the general solution takes the form\n\n$$\ny = e^{2t} \\left( P \\cos 9t + Q \\sin 9t \\right).\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to determine whether each of the given functions takes the form shown above.\n\n- Function I takes the above form with $P=-2$ and $Q=7.$ So, it is a solution to the given ODE.\n\n- Function II does *not* take the above form. It contains $\\sin 3t,$ which is not included in the above form. So, it is *not* a solution to the given ODE.\n\n- Function III takes the above form with $P=0$ and $Q=-6.$ So, it is a solution to the given ODE.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088232 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f271e83c-eb95-5d23-8e02-0039c4c92647" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086047} {:answer/id #uuid "01c305ef-5d62-5370-af62-e640c51bd827" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-1$ or $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086048} {:answer/id #uuid "162972b6-3571-5192-88e8-157c1614a1f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086049} {:answer/id #uuid "1cd9a726-e68f-5f7b-8b94-49a85bfd293a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086050} {:answer/id #uuid "794f04eb-d063-5a2b-bf46-7d9f298f740c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-2$ or $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086051}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186086047} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9338f1b-40c1-5fc3-93b9-76c979df1f32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086046}] :question/id #uuid "dcf8f8dd-2da9-5faf-a261-ff2625b81b2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-64660" :question/problem "Consider the equation $y^{″} + 2y^{′} + y = 0$. If $y = e^{λx}$ is a solution to the equation, then $λ =$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y=e^{\\lambda x}$ gives the characteristic equation\n$$\n\\lambda^2+2\\lambda+1=(\\lambda+1)^2=0.\n$$\nThus $\\lambda=-1$ is a repeated root."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088233 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88d373d4-c665-5bc6-a8b2-e2051e06c5df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4e^{2x} + xe^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086053} {:answer/id #uuid "b26eea1a-9f1e-5a04-93dc-5f1fa0c496c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "xe^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086054} {:answer/id #uuid "d9f64955-0b6d-509a-8d2c-f4db37359f40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6e^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086055} {:answer/id #uuid "4e549842-5564-5b72-803c-c5b40cd5296c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2e^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086056} {:answer/id #uuid "77f97ee5-5d7d-5c8b-b0c2-f59eac307ea1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e^{2x} - 3xe^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086057}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186086056} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc710d54-b7b2-5db2-adb2-c1cf53406ff5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086052}] :question/id #uuid "8501a2ed-546b-52d5-8aa3-8819f259acc1" :question/math-academy-id "q-64662" :question/problem "Given the equation $y^{″} - 4y^{′} + 4y = 0$ for $y = y(x)$, which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?" :question/worked-solution "The characteristic equation is\n$$\n\\lambda^2-4\\lambda+4=(\\lambda-2)^2=0.\n$$\nThe repeated root $\\lambda=2$ gives the general solution\n$$\ny=Ae^{2x}+Bxe^{2x}.\n$$\nAll the given functions have this form except $2e^x.$ Substitution of $y=2e^x$ gives\n$$\ny''-4y'+4y=2e^x-8e^x+8e^x=2e^x\\ne0.\n$$\nTherefore, $2e^x$ is the only non-solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088236 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d28a3b3-9f9e-59dc-88eb-d0379bf9e54f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9e^{x}\\cos 4x + 2e^{x}\\sin 4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086071} {:answer/id #uuid "b4e7844e-9cfb-5bfe-9c0a-351a69bbfe22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8e^{x}\\sin 4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086072} {:answer/id #uuid "baa316df-5472-583b-8418-29664be113d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e^{x}\\cos 4x - 11e^{x}\\sin 4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086073} {:answer/id #uuid "d7a2286e-eff8-5a2f-9b36-1b850f19cc4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5e^{2x}\\sin 4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086074} {:answer/id #uuid "034fce6c-ee50-5a0d-8db5-9c295c214b8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3e^{x}\\cos 4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086075}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186086074} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6503d73-857b-5850-b6df-2fa58ed7c477" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086070}] :question/id #uuid "77282f81-7f46-5bed-8933-2924da5a4281" :question/math-academy-id "q-64697" :question/problem "Given that $y = y(x)$ satisfies the equation $y^{″} - 2y^{′} + 17y = 0$, which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?" :question/worked-solution "The characteristic equation is\n$$\n\\lambda^2-2\\lambda+17=(\\lambda-1)^2+16=0,\n$$\nso the roots are $1\\pm4\\mathrm{i}.$ Hence every solution has the form\n$$\ny=e^x(P\\cos4x+Q\\sin4x).\n$$\nThe only listed function outside this form is $5e^{2x}\\sin4x.$ Indeed,\n$$\ny''-2y'+17y=5e^{2x}(\\sin4x+8\\cos4x)\\ne0.\n$$\nTherefore, $5e^{2x}\\sin4x$ is the only non-solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088237 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b2279f0-fed9-5865-8ed7-d530e203ad5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-10e^{5t}\\sin 2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086077} {:answer/id #uuid "9f4589c9-ce5b-5bb8-be9e-26e7a1ba363f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4e^{5t}\\cos 2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086078} {:answer/id #uuid "b2a97a6b-2ad5-5c4d-85fa-031582d08f87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9e^{5t}\\sin 2t - e^{5t}\\cos 2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086079} {:answer/id #uuid "cabdcdd2-e550-5843-8bb1-99a55b432fee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\sin 2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086080} {:answer/id #uuid "45d9ee41-1278-5ad3-a3a0-90bfdfe12f26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-e^{5t}\\sin 2t + 2e^{5t}\\cos 2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086081}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186086080} :answer-field/id #uuid "37b6d3d4-8658-5bda-9e56-e6fea19665a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086076}] :question/id #uuid "0d4e16b7-05f1-5d1f-9d6e-6ac744b8fa3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-64699" :question/problem "Given that $y = y(t)$ satisfies the equation $y^{″} - 10y^{′} + 29y = 0$, which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?" :question/worked-solution "The characteristic equation is\n$$\n\\lambda^2-10\\lambda+29=(\\lambda-5)^2+4=0,\n$$\nso the roots are $5\\pm2\\mathrm{i}.$ Thus every solution has the form\n$$\ny=e^{5t}(P\\cos2t+Q\\sin2t).\n$$\nThe only listed function outside this form is $5\\sin2t.$ Substitution gives\n$$\ny''-10y'+29y=125\\sin2t-100\\cos2t\\ne0.\n$$\nTherefore, $5\\sin2t$ is the only non-solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088238 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b52bd7be-1c9b-56b8-8b80-8f22fcc65038" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-8$ or $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086083} {:answer/id #uuid "dca0efd6-bade-5c58-82f9-90f3d9a7c961" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-8$ or $8$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086084} {:answer/id #uuid "e98c9f23-c91b-5557-b711-0751bec896b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086085} {:answer/id #uuid "f30ff7b4-102b-52ad-b005-18472