math-academy-id "e-6861" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\ny'' + y' - 2y =0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{x} + Be^{- 2 x},\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'' + y' - 2y =0,\n\\ \\ y(1) = e, \\ \\ y'(1) = e ^{-2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(1)=e$ into our general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e & =A{e}^{1}+B{e}^{-2(1)} \\\\ e & =Ae+B{e}^{-2} \\\\ A & =1-B{e}^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition $y'(1) = e^{-2},$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\ny' = Ae^x - 2Be^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y'(1)=e^{-2}$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2} & =A{e}^{1}-2B{e}^{-2(1)} \\\\ {e}^{-2} & =Ae-2B{e}^{-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations from above together, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}A &=1-B{e}^{-3} \\\\ {e}^{-2} &=Ae-2B{e}^{-2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A = 1 - B e ^{-3}$ in the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2} & =(1-B{e}^{-3})e-2B{e}^{-2} \\\\ 1 & ={e}^{3}-B-2B \\\\ B & =\\frac{{e}^{3}-1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting $B= \\dfrac {e ^ 3 - 1} 3$ back into the equation $A = 1-Be^{-3}$ gives\n\n$$\nA = 1 -  \\dfrac {(e ^ 3 - 1) e ^ {-3}} 3 =   \\dfrac {2 +  e ^ {-3}} 3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution of the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny =  \\dfrac {  e ^ {-3} + 2} 3  e^{x} + \\dfrac {e ^ 3 - 1} 3 e^{-2x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152008 :question/id #uuid "80968044-25e2-5906-929c-b944f84ca27d" :question/math-academy-id "e-7051" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'' - 6y' + 25y=0, \\quad y(0)= 2,  \\quad y'(0) = 5.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the roots of the characteristic polynomial by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +25 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +9)-9+25 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)^{2}+16 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)^{2} & =-16 \\\\ \\lambda -3 & =\\pm 4\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =3\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following general solution:\n\n$$\ny = e^{3x} \\left( P \\cos 4x + Q \\sin 4x \\right)\n$$\n\nWe find the constants $P$ and $Q$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0) = 2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n2 = P.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition $y'(0) = 5,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3{e}^{3x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)+{e}^{3x}(-4P\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+4Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y'(0) = 5$ and $P=2$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =3{e}^{0}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡0)+{e}^{0}(-4(2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+4Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\ 5 & =6+4Q \\\\ -1 & =4Q \\\\ Q & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = e^{3x} \\left( 2 \\cos 4x - \\dfrac{1}{4} \\sin 4x \\right).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152403 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8bfc2fdb-4a42-5994-9537-e2225f8eb1f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y = 7\\cos (\\sqrt{11}x) - \\sqrt{11}\\sin (\\sqrt{11}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148950} {:answer/id #uuid "e229a207-4ef2-5d22-867b-ccab34a24868" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y = 7\\cos (\\sqrt{11}x) - 4\\sin (\\sqrt{11}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148951} {:answer/id #uuid "e946e55a-f081-5877-8dd6-33a721fca4e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y = 7\\cos (\\sqrt{11}x) + 2\\sin (\\sqrt{11}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148952} {:answer/id #uuid "011457b9-c1fa-5467-be44-8de0b082eb4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y = 7\\cos (\\sqrt{11}x) - \\sin (\\sqrt{11}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148953} {:answer/id #uuid "2c46909d-4cc1-5ebf-853b-6b12ed2aabb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y = 7\\cos (\\sqrt{11}x) - \\frac{4\\sqrt{11}}{11}\\sin (\\sqrt{11}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148954}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148951} :answer-field/id #uuid "c923a4d5-1e96-5cfb-9e2d-1463a34bb868" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148949}] :question/id #uuid "31316f4c-9980-5deb-aa03-fe5173062056" :question/math-academy-id "q-130365" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n$$\n\\displaystyle y^{″} + 11y = 0, y(0) = 7, y^{′}(0) =-4\\sqrt{11}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The characteristic roots are $\\lambda=\\pm\\sqrt{11}\\mathrm{i},$ giving\n$$\ny=P\\cos(\\sqrt{11}x)+Q\\sin(\\sqrt{11}x).\n$$\nThe first condition gives $P=7.$ Differentiating,\n$$\ny'=-\\sqrt{11}P\\sin(\\sqrt{11}x)+\\sqrt{11}Q\\cos(\\sqrt{11}x).\n$$\nThus $y'(0)=-4\\sqrt{11}$ gives $\\sqrt{11}Q=-4\\sqrt{11},$ so $Q=-4.$ Therefore,\n$$\ny=7\\cos(\\sqrt{11}x)-4\\sin(\\sqrt{11}x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152404 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d81758a1-467b-530b-82ed-1a512ef72718" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y = e^{-x}(3\\cos 2x - \\sin 2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148956} {:answer/id #uuid "d99d5a15-a222-54c7-bf19-f74127e55acf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y = e^{-x}(3\\cos 2x + \\sin 2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148957} {:answer/id #uuid "0a66397c-0490-5072-82c9-2d2240deff6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y = 3e^{-x}\\cos 2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148958} {:answer/id #uuid "0be5b4db-ddce-59e9-827e-5021dec2fd58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y = e^{-2x}(3\\cos x - 5\\sin x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148959} {:answer/id #uuid "791b2684-a257-53cb-84b7-354943b2e2aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y = 5e^{-2x}\\sin x + 3\\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148960}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148956} :answer-field/id #uuid "90656c46-dd4f-5081-ac12-2c172a198ac4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148955}] :question/id #uuid "10337575-a96a-50d3-819b-452bcfd42d72" :question/math-academy-id "q-130534" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n$$\n\\displaystyle y^{″} + 2y^{′} + 5y = 0, y(0) = 3, y^{′}(0) =-5.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The characteristic equation is\n$$\n\\lambda^2+2\\lambda+5=(\\lambda+1)^2+4=0,\n$$\nwith roots $-1\\pm2\\mathrm{i}.$ Therefore,\n$$\ny=e^{-x}(P\\cos2x+Q\\sin2x).\n$$\nThe condition $y(0)=3$ gives $P=3.$ Differentiating gives\n$$\ny'=-e^{-x}(P\\cos2x+Q\\sin2x)+e^{-x}(-2P\\sin2x+2Q\\cos2x).\n$$\nUsing $y'(0)=-5$ gives $-P+2Q=-5,$ hence $Q=-1.$ Therefore,\n$$\ny=e^{-x}(3\\cos2x-\\sin2x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152779 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b84d0516-abe6-519f-8b0b-3cfa502bafc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y = 5e^{-5x} + 28xe^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151182} {:answer/id #uuid "fecd06bf-6d1f-5b42-93af-6e13a6dc6a3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y = 5e^{-5x} + 20xe^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151183} {:answer/id #uuid "307a4c35-5bf4-51b8-9ebb-a265cf6efbc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y = 5e^{-5x} + 12xe^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151184} {:answer/id #uuid "498ea3a8-9728-5385-968d-996768552371" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y = 5e^{-5x} + 42xe^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151185} {:answer/id #uuid "74d94883-b4ae-54ac-9c75-c13e01009e0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y = 5e^{-5x} + 22xe^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151186}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151185} :answer-field/id #uuid "649939b1-717e-5219-8a42-3f52dce1bd13" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151181}] :question/id #uuid "93338c02-7761-5a7e-bb19-c834b8f78250" :question/math-academy-id "q-7814" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n$y^{″} + 10y^{′} + 25y = 0$\n\nhas the general solution\n$y = Ae^{-5x} + Bxe^{-5x}$,\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n$$\n\\displaystyle y^{″} + 10y^{′} + 25y = 0, y(0) = 5, y^{′}(0) = 17.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The characteristic equation is\n$$\n\\lambda^2+10\\lambda+25=(\\lambda+5)^2=0.\n$$\nThe repeated root $-5$ gives $y=Ae^{-5x}+Bxe^{-5x}.$ From $y(0)=5,$ we obtain $A=5.$ Differentiating gives\n$$\ny'=-5Ae^{-5x}+Be^{-5x}-5Bxe^{-5x}.\n$$\nUsing $y'(0)=17$ yields $-5A+B=17,$ so $B=42.$ Therefore,\n$$\ny=5e^{-5x}+42xe^{-5x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152799 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a308af3-6719-5826-a24a-2f8b7b066053" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y = e^{3x}(5\\cos 5x - \\sin 5x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151302} {:answer/id #uuid "b32ff3e1-6155-5fbd-942d-efef7165e8b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y = e^{3x}(5\\cos 5x + \\sin 5x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151303} {:answer/id #uuid "d969a27a-0ed7-5196-a356-1aa74c7793e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y = e^{3x}(5\\cos 5x - 2\\sin 5x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151304} {:answer/id #uuid "4af2db9f-dff0-536a-80a4-1b8399883298" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y = e^{3x}(5\\cos 5x + 2\\sin 5x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151305} {:answer/id #uuid "5d4f9e59-0cd6-5a6a-961b-b231308bc97f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y = e^{3x}(5\\cos 5x + 3\\sin 5x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151306}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151302} :answer-field/id #uuid "95e0e6f4-3bc8-5f22-a3b9-9a1139d085ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151301}] :question/id #uuid "2270856f-5217-52df-b9ab-69516b7671a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-7895" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n$y^{″} - 6y^{′} + 34y = 0$\n\nhas the general solution\n$y = e^{3x}(P\\cos 5x + Q\\sin 5x)$,\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n$$\n\\displaystyle y^{″} - 6y^{′} + 34y = 0, y(0) = 5, y^{′}(0) = 10.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The characteristic roots are $3\\pm5\\mathrm{i},$ so\n$$\ny=e^{3x}(P\\cos5x+Q\\sin5x).\n$$\nThe first condition gives $P=5.$ Differentiating,\n$$\ny'=3e^{3x}(P\\cos5x+Q\\sin5x)+e^{3x}(-5P\\sin5x+5Q\\cos5x).\n$$\nUsing $y'(0)=10$ gives $3P+5Q=10,$ hence $Q=-1.$ Therefore,\n$$\ny=e^{3x}(5\\cos5x-\\sin5x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335759 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c237fa7-ac00-545d-b965-36b324fc7269" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y(t)=2{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333555} {:answer/id #uuid "3e7d624d-752f-5db5-b650-3f8a21a8e760" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y(t)={t}^{2}-4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333579} {:answer/id #uuid "522f952e-c9bd-5980-b216-1eda84eba544" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y(t)=4{t}^{3}-t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333745} {:answer/id #uuid "5397d470-aafa-5976-93a5-86443b26b39a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y(t)={t}^{3}-4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333752} {:answer/id #uuid "7e6bdd13-9626-540d-b255-d0235484bbff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y(t)=3{t}^{2}+2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334117}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333752} :answer-field/id #uuid "507cf8be-acb2-5c6d-9b9e-4af4a627f069" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335394}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3d3a6a7a-d03e-5147-a788-c15fe934645d" :question/math-academy-id "q-64897" :question/problem "Find the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}{y}^{″}-3t{y}^{′}+3y=0,\\,y(2)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=8,\\,t>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={t}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}-3t\\cdot \\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1}+3{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda }-3\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda }+3{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {t}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-3\\lambda +3] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-3\\lambda +3 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +3 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda -3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the roots are $\\lambda =1$ and $\\lambda =3.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)={c}_{1}t+{c}_{2}{t}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$\n\nSubstituting $y(2)=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n0=2{c}_{1}+8{c}_{2}\\,⇒\\,{c}_{1}=-4{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(2)=8.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={c}_{1}+3{c}_{2}{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(2)=8$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & ={c}_{1}+3{c}_{2}(2)^{2} \\\\ 8 & ={c}_{1}+12{c}_{2} \\\\ 8 & =-4{c}_{2}+12{c}_{2} \\\\ 8 & =8{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & =1\\,⇒\\,{c}_{1}=-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(t)={t}^{3}-4t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345349 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20709554-99f3-56be-8db0-e0eb104e22da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=4{e}^{-3x}-2{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342505} {:answer/id #uuid "84bbff61-6dd6-5dae-8917-44ead432bf05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=5{e}^{-2x+2}-3{e}^{7x-7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343373} {:answer/id #uuid "974b02d5-ce48-565e-ac84-17370d67e1a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={e}^{-2x+2}+{e}^{7x-7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343565} {:answer/id #uuid "a2f6aab0-8368-5237-9d3f-acd4dd4686c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=4{e}^{-2x+2}-2{e}^{7x-7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343668} {:answer/id #uuid "d8253bb3-ebb4-55c1-b1e5-02305a7dfdb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={e}^{3x-3}+{e}^{4x-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344140}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343565} :answer-field/id #uuid "be023620-9f23-57b8-b330-3195dfdaa3b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344827}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fdf2426a-3d50-5569-9ef4-40597922e38a" :question/math-academy-id "q-7790" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-5{y}^{′}-14y=0,\\,y(1)=2,\\text{ }\\text{ }{y}^{′}(1)=5.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-5\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}-14{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda -14) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda -14 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda +2)(\\lambda -7)=0,\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =-2$ or $\\lambda =7.$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{7x}.\n$$\n\nNow let us find constants $A$ and $B.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(1)=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA{e}^{-2}+B{e}^{7}=2.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=5.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-2A{e}^{-2x}+7B{e}^{7x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=5$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n-2A{e}^{-2}+7B{e}^{7}=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations for $A$ and $B$:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{-2}+B{e}^{7} &=2 \\\\ -2A{e}^{-2}+7B{e}^{7} &=5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A={e}^{2},B={e}^{-7}.$\n\nNow we get the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{-2x}+{e}^{-7}\\cdot {e}^{7x} \\\\ y & ={e}^{-2x+2}+{e}^{7x-7}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348067 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29e8157a-552f-5c3e-9922-5f98c42236bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-2{e}^{x/2-1}+3x{e}^{x/2-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345723} {:answer/id #uuid "2aa6a344-1c11-5d1d-91e2-7b17d052e60c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=x{e}^{-3x/2+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345730} {:answer/id #uuid "3fb0744b-1328-5467-aff8-5eda9102546f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-{e}^{-3x/2+1}+2x{e}^{-3x/2+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345893} {:answer/id #uuid "5e98c6cd-0b58-542c-859e-0b8cec34dba1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-{e}^{x/2-1}+2x{e}^{x/2-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346167} {:answer/id #uuid "8abde012-2b17-5b1a-afc7-33d35b8535b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=3{e}^{-3x/2+1}-2x{e}^{-3x/2+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346552}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345893} :answer-field/id #uuid "db589b01-39df-5ab0-9143-384f9d7d07fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347992}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ece2165a-867c-5bfa-9095-1c580456d3cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-7815" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+\\frac{9y}{4}=0,\\,y(1)={e}^{-1/2},\\text{ }\\text{ }{y}^{′}(1)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-1/2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+3\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+\\frac{9}{4}{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}\\left({\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda +\\frac{9}{4}\\right) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda +\\frac{9}{4} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left(\\lambda +\\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\left(\\lambda +\\frac{3}{2}\\right) & =0 \\\\ {\\left(\\lambda +\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\lambda =-\\frac{3}{2}$ is a repeated root.\n\nSo the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x/2}+Bx{e}^{-3x/2}.\n$$\n\nLet us find constants $A$ and $B.$ \nSubstituting $y(1)={e}^{-1/2}$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{-3/2}+B{e}^{-3/2} & ={e}^{-1/2} \\\\ A+B & =e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-1/2}.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\frac{3}{2}A{e}^{-3/2x}+B{e}^{-3/2x}-\\frac{3}{2}Bx{e}^{-3/2x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-1/2}$ into the above gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2}A{e}^{-3/2}-\\frac{1}{2}B{e}^{-3/2} & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-1/2} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}A-\\frac{1}{2}B & =\\frac{1}{2}e \\\\ -3A-B & =e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations for $A$ and $B$:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}A+B &=e \\\\ -3A-B &=e\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=-e,B=2e.$\n\nNow we get the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-e\\cdot {e}^{-3x/2}+2e\\cdot x{e}^{-3x/2} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-3x/2+1}+2x{e}^{-3x/2+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357596 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05bf703b-213d-5853-963f-5bd3b0167d41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y(x)=2x-5x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354702} {:answer/id #uuid "6ed22a9e-d85f-5a1e-9356-d2763ae75f59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y(x)=\\frac{x}{2}-2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355646} {:answer/id #uuid "90a4e36c-4a86-5551-a5a1-0d04491243dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y(x)=x-4x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355940} {:answer/id #uuid "ca5bb300-699d-5967-b650-dc9d7bdc1238" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y(x)=x-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356429} {:answer/id #uuid "d91209b9-7435-577c-9930-c1a0600d9833" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y(x)=x+3x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356570}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356570} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5bbe535-449b-5b39-b27a-6d5a07c96c23" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357325}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9340f8a4-c84d-5e82-96e6-14527bafdc56" :question/math-academy-id "q-93910" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny={c}_{1}x+{c}_{2}x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+y=0,\\,y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=4,\\,x>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- First, we apply the condition $y(1)=1.$ Substituting $y(1)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n1={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\n- Then, we apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=4.$ Differentiating $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={c}_{1}+{c}_{2}+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=4$ and ${c}_{1}=1$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =1+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(x)=x+3x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357599 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26503b29-5d97-5698-9856-731c91189fd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y(t)=3{t}^{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354987} {:answer/id #uuid "9ecd89cb-8c63-5ac6-b87d-ef24046664ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y(t)=2{t}^{9}+3{t}^{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356078} {:answer/id #uuid "aaaaa9a6-e18f-5b43-b062-909befb9e899" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y(t)={t}^{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356174} {:answer/id #uuid "ae962cc9-644f-5bd8-ba08-86df2433cb67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y(t)=4{t}^{9}-3{t}^{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356202} {:answer/id #uuid "e7a2d3a4-dd04-59ac-bc32-b5db7f6b6faf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y(t)=-2{t}^{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356689}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354987} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d43ad62-447f-5154-9458-2b8539dd8b1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357140}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "bd788041-dc66-5514-9b2b-394d5d0e328a" :question/math-academy-id "q-93915" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}{y}^{″}-17t{y}^{′}+81y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny={c}_{1}{t}^{9}+{c}_{2}{t}^{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}{y}^{″}-17t{y}^{′}+81y=0,\\,y(1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=3,\\,t>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- First, we apply the condition $y(1)=0.$ Substituting $y(1)=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n0={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\n- Then, we apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=3.$ Differentiating $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =9{c}_{1}{t}^{8}+{c}_{2}{t}^{8}+9{c}_{2}{t}^{8}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=3$ and ${c}_{1}=0$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =9{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ 3 & =0+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =3{t}^{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370979 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64f9155f-b8e2-56b7-a158-a740a6de2702" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y(x)={x}^{3/2}+{x}^{-1/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370981} {:answer/id #uuid "f262bd61-3dc4-5968-8c17-fd17918dc225" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y(x)=-2{x}^{-3/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370982} {:answer/id #uuid "3ce6dd4d-4978-575d-a364-ed5c7305aba0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y(x)=2{x}^{1/2}+{x}^{-1/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370983} {:answer/id #uuid "ee1ce27c-13b8-521a-bc8e-67a21be77e71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y(x)=-2{x}^{1/2}+{x}^{-3/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370984} {:answer/id #uuid "2a3911dc-c3c9-537b-9c6f-438d0ce84acd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y(x)={x}^{1/2}-{x}^{-3/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370985}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370985} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8d3595b-7aa5-5180-9aed-06125c911386" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370980}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7fa70513-33ac-562e-accc-6e741a539b11" :question/math-academy-id "q-64841" :question/problem "Find the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+8x{y}^{′}-3y=0,\\,y(1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=2,\\,x>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={x}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}+8x\\cdot \\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1}-3{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 4\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda }+8\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda }-3{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda }[4\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+8\\lambda -3] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+8\\lambda -3 & =0 \\\\ 4{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda -3 & =0 \\\\ (2\\lambda -1)(2\\lambda +3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the roots are $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\lambda =-\\frac{3}{2}.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{1/2}+{c}_{2}{x}^{-3/2}.\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$\n\nSubstituting $y(1)=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n0={c}_{1}+{c}_{2}\\,⇒\\,{c}_{2}=-{c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=2.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{c}_{1}{x}^{-1/2}}{2}-\\frac{3{c}_{2}{x}^{-5/2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=2$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =\\frac{{c}_{1}}{2}-\\frac{3{c}_{2}}{2} \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{{c}_{1}}{2}-\\frac{3(-{c}_{1})}{2} \\\\ 2 & =2{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{1} & =1\\,⇒\\,{c}_{2}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(x)={x}^{1/2}-{x}^{-3/2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393765 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eeaa8919-6cd1-523d-89da-7ef2030fd077" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=11{e}^{4x-4}-9x{e}^{4x-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393767} {:answer/id #uuid "31ad293a-a2de-5b04-a010-75b1e574916b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-7{e}^{4x-4}+9x{e}^{4x-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393768} {:answer/id #uuid "0a7466a7-5e6b-57ce-b40f-1d86f392fa6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=5{e}^{4x-4}-3x{e}^{4x-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393769} {:answer/id #uuid "2731b0a9-3ca5-56a5-b3d2-8efcf3d3f0af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=8{e}^{2x-2}-6x{e}^{2x-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393770} {:answer/id #uuid "738f5cba-3a2c-5ec8-92f7-05586e62d819" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=3{e}^{2x-2}-x{e}^{2x-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393771}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393767} :answer-field/id #uuid "656ffe0f-9fb3-55a1-98b5-b13db8a4f14b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393766}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e0ccd8f3-5c55-5b1e-8569-1f7045c0f609" :question/math-academy-id "q-7798" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-8{y}^{′}+16y=0,\\,y(1)=2,\\text{ }\\text{ }{y}^{′}(1)=-1.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-8\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+16{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +16) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +16 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda -4)(\\lambda -4) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -4)^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\lambda =4$ is a repeated root.\n\nSo the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{4x}+Bx{e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nLet us find constants $A$ and $B.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(1)=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA{e}^{4}+B{e}^{4}=2.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=-1.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4A{e}^{4x}+B{e}^{4x}+4Bx{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=-1$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n4A{e}^{4}+5B{e}^{4}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations for $A$ and $B$:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{4}+B{e}^{4} &=2 \\\\ 4A{e}^{4}+5B{e}^{4} &=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=11{e}^{-4},B=-9{e}^{-4}.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =11{e}^{-4}\\cdot {e}^{4x}-9{e}^{-4}\\cdot x{e}^{4x} \\\\  & =11{e}^{4x-4}-9x{e}^{4x-4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431532 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b282211-1d23-5349-b3f7-ddda48199c41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={e}^{x-\\pi /6}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431468} {:answer/id #uuid "50868c33-8407-589c-bfad-291f32fb6f3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={e}^{-x+\\pi /6}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431477} {:answer/id #uuid "9fa32848-af4f-5ffd-ad94-45426b415ba6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={e}^{x-\\pi /6}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431492} {:answer/id #uuid "bf0206d8-67a6-5880-99ad-0ae8bdd971eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-{e}^{-x+\\pi /6}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431502} {:answer/id #uuid "bfb9cf92-1a16-58f2-9b54-96a4a29652b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-{e}^{-x+\\pi /6}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431503}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431477} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4eeea1c-8cea-5fa0-af41-255c4c40e45b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431529}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1a78761c-d0ad-5651-b02a-e973e2385ada" :question/math-academy-id "q-7893" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+10y=0,\\,y\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)=1,\\text{ }\\text{ }{y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)=2.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+2\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+10{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +10) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +10=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +10 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +1)-1+10 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2}+9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2} & =-9 \\\\ \\lambda +1 & =\\pm 3\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-1\\pm 3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{-x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants $P$ and $Q.$ \n\nSubstituting $y\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q{e}^{-\\pi /6} & =1 \\\\ Q & ={e}^{\\pi /6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)=2.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-{e}^{-x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)+{e}^{-x}(-3P\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+3Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)=2$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-Q{e}^{-\\pi /6}-3P{e}^{-\\pi /6} & =2 \\\\ -Q-3P & =2{e}^{\\pi /6} \\\\ -{e}^{\\pi /6}-3P & =2{e}^{\\pi /6} \\\\ P & =-{e}^{\\pi /6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $Q={e}^{\\pi /6}$ and $P=-{e}^{\\pi /6}.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{-x}(-{e}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+{e}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x) \\\\  & ={e}^{-x+\\pi /6}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431535 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0db04b57-7e0b-51b5-9068-627b530d93d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-2{e}^{x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431461} {:answer/id #uuid "697fcea0-b422-5fd9-8a3c-b4de3653327a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-2{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431482} {:answer/id #uuid "a8ec0f9e-bd19-5a45-8045-94296328023e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-2{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431494} {:answer/id #uuid "c75b25a4-08bf-5b34-b13b-1d43f8125742" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-2{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-4{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431506} {:answer/id #uuid "c906702c-bb29-5e13-834a-3f940496c811" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-2{e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431507}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431482} :answer-field/id #uuid "aab4196f-6b76-5bce-9655-3bb0e187a2a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431527}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4d183a38-3bd7-5a3d-b0c0-6bbf656b9b64" :question/math-academy-id "q-130533" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}+2y=0,\\,y(0)=-2,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=-4.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-2\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+2{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +2=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +2 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +1)-1+2 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ \\lambda -1 & =\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =1\\pm \\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants $P$ and $Q.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(0)=-2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nP=-2.\n$$\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\ny={e}^{x}(-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=-4.$ We differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={e}^{x}(-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+{e}^{x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=-4$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n-2+Q=-4\\,⟹\\,Q=-2.\n$$\n\nSo we have $P=-2$ and $Q=-2.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-2{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186645757 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14aed398-91a9-5961-b792-888c19f32532" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y(t)=\\frac{1}{t}-{t}^{1/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186645175} {:answer/id #uuid "40f6d6d3-c741-5b1b-b40b-94765fecf702" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y(t)=\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}}+{t}^{3/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186645242} {:answer/id #uuid "4b350a9f-a570-5e1c-86bb-98520bbe28ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y(t)=-\\frac{2}{t}+4{t}^{3/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186645268} {:answer/id #uuid "b33c8422-d6ac-55c3-b0a4-26221bcdd076" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y(t)=\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}-2{t}^{1/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186645449} {:answer/id #uuid "bf713147-cdd7-550b-827a-734239ee5dfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y(t)=-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}+2{t}^{3/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186645475}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186645475} :answer-field/id #uuid "4faf34d8-a43b-53b9-b61d-7783a1129793" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186645603}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c8ec0e5f-29bb-5ff9-bed2-44af7b2e00cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-64903" :question/problem "Find the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n2{t}^{2}{y}^{″}+3t{y}^{′}-6y=0,\\,y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=5,\\,t>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={t}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}+3t\\cdot \\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1}-6{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 2\\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda }+3\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda }-6{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {t}^{\\lambda }[2\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+3\\lambda -6] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+3\\lambda -6 & =0 \\\\ 2{\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -6 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)(2\\lambda -3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the roots are $\\lambda =-2$ and $\\lambda =\\frac{3}{2}.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)={c}_{1}{t}^{-2}+{c}_{2}{t}^{3/2}.\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$\n\nSubstituting $y(1)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n1={c}_{1}+{c}_{2}\\,⇒\\,{c}_{2}=1-{c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=5.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-2{c}_{1}{t}^{-3}+\\frac{3}{2}{c}_{2}{t}^{1/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=5$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =-2{c}_{1}+\\frac{3}{2}{c}_{2} \\\\ 5 & =-2{c}_{1}+\\frac{3}{2}(1-{c}_{1}) \\\\ \\frac{7}{2} & =-\\frac{7}{2}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{1} & =-1\\,⇒\\,{c}_{2}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(t)=-{t}^{-2}+2{t}^{3/2}=-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}+2{t}^{3/2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186649848 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a834485-f79b-5f15-bdce-7165deae935a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y(t)=\\frac{3t-9\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{{t}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186649513} {:answer/id #uuid "8eee944d-4694-5a58-af06-09fd84f98f32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y(t)=\\frac{3+15\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{{t}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186649525} {:answer/id #uuid "a9040967-da70-51ab-ab3b-5d9a1e768c64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y(t)={t}^{4}(3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186649574} {:answer/id #uuid "f7c7764e-adaa-578f-9df1-df826b8efdfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y(t)=\\frac{3+9\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{{t}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186649699} {:answer/id #uuid "fcbfb18c-cad4-5279-9265-2fd252fb7d12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y(t)={t}^{4}(15+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186649707}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186649525} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd568edd-d2f7-58c4-a520-a1d65a66b00a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186649791}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5d4dd6b2-e55e-56ba-ba04-ff57addd7ee7" :question/math-academy-id "q-64884" :question/problem "Find the solution of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}{y}^{″}+9t{y}^{′}+16y=0,\\,y(1)=3,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=3,\\,t>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={t}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}+9t\\cdot \\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1}+16{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda }+9\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda }+16{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {t}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+9\\lambda +16] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+9\\lambda +16 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +16 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we have a double root $\\lambda =-4.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)={c}_{1}{t}^{-4}+{c}_{2}{t}^{-4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{{t}^{4}}+\\frac{{c}_{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{{t}^{4}}.\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$\n\nSubstituting $y(1)=3$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n3={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=3.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-\\frac{4{c}_{1}}{{t}^{5}}+\\frac{{c}_{2}}{{t}^{5}}-\\frac{4{c}_{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)}{{t}^{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=3$ and ${c}_{1}=3$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =-4{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ 3 & =-12+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\frac{3+15\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{{t}^{4}}.\n$$"}]}
